--TEST--
A closure `Core\Fatal::onLimit` holds is still an ordinary `callable` the program may call and pass on — `rule:errors/on-limit`, `rule:types/callable-is-the-only-function-type`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `rule:types/callable-is-the-only-function-type` gives the language one closure type, and registering one as a
// tier-1 handler is a *use* of it rather than a conversion into something else.
// The boundary this asks about is ownership: the context takes a reference of
// its own (`Ctx::set_limit_handler`), so a registration that moved the closure
// instead — or that took none and left the program holding a value the request
// would later release under it — fails here rather than at a breach nobody has
// provoked yet.
var $label = "held";
var $handler = fn (): void => { echo "handler says ", $label, "\n"; };

Core\Fatal::onLimit($handler);
$handler();
$handler();

// The same value in three roles at once: registered, called by name, and beside
// a member that takes a `callable`. A closure the registration had consumed
// answers none of these, and one whose capture the registration had taken over
// prints something other than what it captured.
Core\Fatal::onLimit($handler);
var $doubled = Core\Arr::map([1, 2, 3], fn (int $n): int => $n * 2);
echo "map still works: ", Core\Json::encode($doubled), "\n";
$handler();
--EXPECT--
handler says held
handler says held
map still works: [2,4,6]
handler says held
